1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,080
The school weekend combines advanced respiratory reading with complex
cardiac and multisystem practice. This rehearsal brings those
requirements together rather than replacing them with another
unrelated chapter list.

2
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:25,008
Use the earlier diagnostic, respiratory and twelve-lead sessions as
working tools. Practise what you assess, say, do and reassess when new
information appears.

3
00:00:25,008 --> 00:00:37,320
The original cases here follow the school objectives without
reproducing a school quiz or submitted response. Use a tabletop,
training equipment or an observed classroom exercise as appropriate.

4
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:48,960
Treat immediate threats while explaining uncertainty. The purpose is
executable clinical reasoning, not reciting a string of diagnosis
names after the patient deteriorates.

5
00:00:48,960 --> 00:01:02,424
AP, FON and MSN explain gas transport and acid-base processes. Use
them to describe a pattern and its limits, not to turn every low pH
into one metabolic diagnosis.

6
00:01:02,424 --> 00:01:15,096
BCEHS and BCPEG supply separately identified procedure and examination
wording. Keep different revisions, targets and eligibility decisions
visible rather than averaging them.

7
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:27,984
CV and PHARM connect conduction, ischemia and medication risk; CNS
links fluid/electrolyte assessment, respiratory support and infusion
safety to the same patient.

8
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:42,000
CPSC and VSM teach equipment and trustworthy observations; PMHN
prevents diagnostic overshadowing. Nancy remains the main textbook
bridge, not a substitute for those eleven assigned rows.

9
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:51,792
Before class, explain the advanced ECG patterns without claiming new
LBBB alone proves MI or every high-lateral event is an equivalent.

10
00:01:51,792 --> 00:02:03,144
Reconstruct the complete CPAP and PEEP screens and stop plans. If the
patient no longer meets the functional requirements, change support
rather than defend the first choice.

11
00:02:03,144 --> 00:02:13,464
Explain why saturation, carbon dioxide and pressure describe different
parts of the system. A good value in one part cannot guarantee the
others are safe.

12
00:02:13,464 --> 00:02:23,712
Practise a complete alert with a timer, and practise a deterioration
interrupting your plan. Do not rehearse only the version in which
every treatment works.

13
00:02:23,712 --> 00:03:08,088
Apply the assigned counting technique to a suitable observation, not
as a delay in an emergency. One breath is the entire inspiration-
expiration cycle. In a regular resting pattern the source counts
thirty seconds and doubles it; with an irregular rhythm it counts a
full minute, and it uses a full minute for infants. Observe chest and
abdominal movement, symmetry, depth, sounds and effort. Explain and
document relevant crying, movement, pain or activity rather than
calling that an unqualified resting value. In the weekly deteriorating
patient, recognize ineffective breathing and support it immediately
instead of finishing a routine count while the patient fails.

14
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:19,200
The team lead states the main threat and immediate priorities. Shared
understanding is more useful than several providers independently
pursuing their favorite diagnosis.

15
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:27,528
Assign airway and breathing support explicitly so it does not
disappear while an ECG is printed or an alert is delivered.

16
00:03:27,528 --> 00:03:38,760
Assign circulation, hemorrhage and the usable ECG with clear feedback.
Ask for the actual finding and time, not merely whether the task is
done.

17
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:48,624
Use closed-loop communication for changes and requests. When a
provider notices a discrepant trend, the team must hear and reassess
it promptly.

18
00:03:48,624 --> 00:04:00,696
The primary threat is the problem most immediately endangering airway,
breathing or circulation. It is not automatically the oldest diagnosis
or the most dramatic ECG label.

19
00:04:00,696 --> 00:04:11,016
A complicating condition changes reserve or the safety of treatment.
Chronic cardiac disease may complicate hemorrhage without making blood
loss unimportant.

20
00:04:11,016 --> 00:04:21,336
The cause of an incident is another question. A medical event might
precede a collision, yet uncontrolled traumatic bleeding may be the
immediate priority afterward.

21
00:04:21,336 --> 00:04:29,520
State each separately and update the priority when observations
change. More than one problem can need parallel care.

22
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:39,768
Before a planned intervention, state the working explanation and a
dangerous alternative. The school wants this reasoning verbalized, not
kept invisible.

23
00:04:39,768 --> 00:04:49,104
Choose an alternative that could change care, such as hemorrhage
versus pump failure, pneumothorax versus edema or hypoventilation
versus anxiety.

24
00:04:49,104 --> 00:04:59,016
Name a useful discriminator and the associated next action. A long
unranked list that cannot change the plan is not a good differential
check.

25
00:04:59,016 --> 00:05:10,032
Do not let the spoken check postpone immediate hemorrhage control,
ventilation or arrest care. Parallel reasoning and rescue are safer
than a ceremonial pause.

26
00:05:10,032 --> 00:05:20,328
First state what actually changed in breathing, mentation, perfusion
or symptoms. A device setting change is not itself a patient response.

27
00:05:20,328 --> 00:05:30,528
Next compare that observation with what the treatment should have
changed and over what time. A slow anti-inflammatory effect should not
be expected in seconds.

28
00:05:30,528 --> 00:05:41,736
Ask what worsened, including pressure after positive pressure or
altered mentation despite improved saturation. An improved comfort
goal can coexist with physiological harm.

29
00:05:41,736 --> 00:05:52,464
Then make the next decision explicit: continue, adjust, stop or
escalate. Reassessment only counts when the finding can influence
care.

30
00:05:52,464 --> 00:06:03,768
A sixty-six-year-old at a community repair workshop has been
resuscitated by a trained team. Assume ROSC is confirmed and
appropriate ongoing support is in place.

31
00:06:03,768 --> 00:06:17,520
At the first reference point, ETCO2 is thirty-six, saturation ninety-
seven percent and pressure one hundred four over sixty-eight. Record
the actual airway and ventilation state with those values.

32
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:28,320
A single acceptable snapshot does not guarantee the next minute.
Continue serial assessment and prepare the appropriate post-arrest ECG
and conveyance pathway.

33
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:38,880
Assign who watches the trend and who announces a change. The most
important information may be the difference from this baseline, not a
new isolated threshold.

34
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:51,216
Several minutes later ETCO2 falls to eighteen, saturation remains
ninety-six and pressure is seventy-eight over forty-four.
Responsiveness and perfusion also worsen.

35
00:06:51,216 --> 00:07:02,736
The acceptable oxygen percentage must not hide the circulatory threat.
Reassess airway, effective breathing, pulse and the possibility of re-
arrest promptly.

36
00:07:02,736 --> 00:07:13,224
Check current support and the sampling circuit while necessary care
continues. Treat the credible physiological deterioration rather than
only troubleshooting a number.

37
00:07:13,224 --> 00:07:25,272
Do not reflexively ventilate faster because CO2 is low. If pulmonary
blood flow has fallen, more ventilation can further impair the balance
and miss the real problem.

38
00:07:25,272 --> 00:07:37,296
ETCO2 depends on carbon-dioxide production, delivery to the lungs,
effective ventilation and measurement. A sudden fall does not identify
one mechanism by itself.

39
00:07:37,296 --> 00:07:48,048
Poor perfusion can reduce carbon dioxide reaching the lungs. In this
case, falling pressure and responsiveness make that branch
particularly urgent.

40
00:07:48,048 --> 00:08:01,680
Changes in ventilation or the airway/circuit can also lower the
result. Inspect the waveform, chest movement, air entry, support
settings and connections rather than assuming unchanged function.

41
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:14,448
If arrest recurs, use the actual arrest pathway immediately. If a
pulse remains, support the actual shock/respiratory problem and
escalate without waiting for another gas.

42
00:08:14,448 --> 00:08:25,728
For the school Wednesday exercise, state its ninety-four-to-ninety-
eight-percent target. Do not describe a saturation of one hundred as
the goal simply because ROSC occurred.

43
00:08:25,728 --> 00:08:38,040
The checked N03 has different wording within its essentials and
detailed interventions. Those differences are retained in the Week
Five reading, not averaged into a new invented range.

44
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:49,272
Correct immediate hypoxemia and use reliable monitoring to titrate
through the actual pathway. Avoid unnecessary excessive oxygen without
withholding needed treatment.

45
00:08:49,272 --> 00:09:00,696
A target saturation cannot establish adequate ventilation, hemoglobin
carriage or circulation. In Case A, a good percentage does not solve
the falling pressure.

46
00:09:00,696 --> 00:09:12,840
Use appropriate effective ventilation and avoid excess rate or volume.
An ETCO2 target assumes a valid measurement and enough pulmonary
perfusion to interpret it.

47
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:26,136
ETCO2 and arterial carbon dioxide are not always interchangeable,
especially when perfusion or gas exchange changes. A field end-tidal
number is not an arterial blood gas.

48
00:09:26,136 --> 00:09:39,624
Assess pressure and the broader perfusion picture. The checked N03
uses different pressure and carbon-dioxide wording in general versus
advanced sections; keep the actual role-specific pathway explicit.

49
00:09:39,624 --> 00:09:50,760
Do not invent an automatic fluid or vasopressor order from a classroom
target. Treat the likely mechanism, check response and coordinate the
appropriate next capability.

50
00:09:50,760 --> 00:10:01,560
Coordinate the post-ROSC ECG while continuing physiological support.
Do not stop watching pressure and breathing because the team has
reached an electrical task.

51
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:16,632
Record acquisition timing relative to ROSC and important
interventions. The saved school and current N03 timing language is not
identical; use the named pathway rather than inventing a new combined
clock.

52
00:10:16,632 --> 00:10:28,392
Review the whole trace for ischemia and rhythm, including posterior
clues, high lateral and conduction-related limitations. New LBBB alone
is still not diagnostic.

53
00:10:28,392 --> 00:10:39,288
Communicate concerning findings and instability promptly. Do not wait
for troponin or a perfectly certain culprit vessel before requesting
the appropriate receiving capability.

54
00:10:39,288 --> 00:10:51,024
Choose movement and conveyance through the actual pathway while
continuing support. A transport plan must preserve access to the
airway and the ability to respond to re-arrest.

55
00:10:51,024 --> 00:11:02,496
Check oxygen reserve, power, connections and monitoring before moving,
then recheck afterward. Motion can disturb both treatment delivery and
the apparent measurements.

56
00:11:02,496 --> 00:11:14,328
Secure lines without traction, keep required drainage orientation and
have the appropriate backup ready. A neatly strapped patient can still
have a disconnected respiratory circuit.

57
00:11:14,328 --> 00:11:23,184
Update the receiving team if support requirements or stability change.
Give the current problem, not only the condition when the first alert
was sent.

58
00:11:23,184 --> 00:11:34,848
A sixty-one-year-old driver is injured in a high-energy collision. A
witness is unsure whether the driver became unwell before impact, and
a cardiac history is reported.

59
00:11:34,848 --> 00:11:45,552
There is major external bleeding and poor perfusion now. Control the
immediate traumatic threat while arranging appropriate airway,
breathing and circulation care.

60
00:11:45,552 --> 00:11:57,264
A cardiac event could have triggered the incident or coexist, but it
does not explain away the blood loss. The cause of the crash and the
present priority are distinct questions.

61
00:11:57,264 --> 00:12:06,288
Use parallel team tasks to obtain relevant history and a useful ECG
without delaying hemorrhage control or necessary conveyance.

62
00:12:06,288 --> 00:12:16,872
Use the established trauma approach, scene safety and appropriate
resources. Do not narrow the encounter to an ECG because the patient
has a cardiac history.

63
00:12:16,872 --> 00:12:27,408
Control catastrophic bleeding and support airway, breathing and
circulation. A spoken differential should happen alongside rescue, not
postpone it.

64
00:12:27,408 --> 00:12:39,096
Consider hidden hemorrhage, chest injury and other traumatic causes of
shock through the actual indications and role. Positive pressure and
medications can change risk in an injured patient.

65
00:12:39,096 --> 00:12:50,664
Reassess the response and coordinate the receiving capability for the
combined problem. Do not perform an unnecessary prolonged
investigation to prove which event occurred first.

66
00:12:50,664 --> 00:13:01,368
Suppose a useful trace raises ischemic concern. Preserve and
communicate it while continuing trauma care; neither evidence stream
should erase the other.

67
00:13:01,368 --> 00:13:13,584
Before a cardiac medicine, check actual indications and exclusions,
including active bleeding, pressure, volume status and the relevant
interaction or consultation requirements.

68
00:13:13,584 --> 00:13:22,536
Do not reflexively give a nitrate to a poorly perfused bleeding
patient or suppress compensatory tachycardia because the monitor rate
is high.

69
00:13:22,536 --> 00:13:33,648
State what is immediately primary, what is complicating and what
remains uncertain. Coordinate the actual combined destination and
escalation pathway.

70
00:13:33,648 --> 00:13:42,792
Ask brief questions that can change care, including symptom timing,
collapse before impact, medications and relevant baseline function.

71
00:13:42,792 --> 00:13:54,408
Use witnesses, records or device information when available, but
identify the source. A family report and a measured observation are
not the same kind of evidence.

72
00:13:54,408 --> 00:14:04,920
State uncertainty accurately: unknown anticoagulant use is not no
anticoagulant, and no prior ECG is not a definitely new bundle block.

73
00:14:04,920 --> 00:14:15,720
Do not search belongings or pursue a complete history while immediate
threats remain unsupported. Hand over what is missing so the next team
can continue the investigation.

74
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:25,776
A patient may be anxious because breathing or circulation is failing.
Respectful engagement is useful, but it is not proof that the cause is
psychological.

75
00:14:25,776 --> 00:14:37,368
New altered mentation requires the current medical assessment,
including relevant oxygenation, ventilation, glucose, perfusion and
medicine/substance context.

76
00:14:37,368 --> 00:14:48,624
Use the assigned PMHN framework to separate observation from diagnosis
and avoid overshadowing. DSM classification does not replace urgent
physical care.

77
00:14:48,624 --> 00:15:01,176
Calmer, cooperative or refusing are descriptions, not automatic
statements about stability or decision-making capacity. Assess those
questions through the actual clinical and legal process.

78
00:15:01,176 --> 00:15:50,352
Keep the named U.S. organizations attached to their textbook
functions. ANA develops general professional nursing standards; APNA
and ISPN address psychiatric-mental-health nursing standards. NCSBN
brings nursing regulators together, while the actual jurisdictional
board enforces its Nurse Practice Act and makes licensure or
disciplinary decisions. NLN emphasizes nursing education; AACN
develops education competencies, and CCNE, ACEN and CNEA are named
accreditation bodies. A professional association, school accreditor,
employer and statutory regulator are not interchangeable. These names
help you understand the assigned reading, not identify the authority
governing a BC paramedic.

79
00:15:50,352 --> 00:16:36,960
Match the six nursing-process terms to the six CJMM steps: assessment
to recognizing cues; diagnosis to analyzing cues; outcomes
identification to prioritizing hypotheses; planning to generating
solutions; implementation to taking action; evaluation to evaluating
outcomes. This is the source table's comparison, not a rule that every
real decision fits one box. Coordination of care and health teaching
sit within implementation. In an original breathlessness rehearsal,
collect the changing effort and pressure, compare explanations,
prioritize the immediate threat, plan support, act and check the
response; restart the cycle when observations disagree.

80
00:16:36,960 --> 00:17:30,432
Recall the six QSEN areas with their practical meaning: person-
centered care hears the person's goals; teamwork shares a discrepant
finding; evidence-based practice checks a claim; quality improvement
examines a repeatable failure; safety prevents avoidable harm;
informatics preserves reliable information. The reading also names HHS
for broad U.S. health programs, CMS for Medicare/Medicaid
administration, Healthy People 2030 for population goals, OSHA for
worker safety and the Joint Commission for organizational quality
accreditation. Its patient-safety examples include two identifiers,
handover, medication checks, infection prevention, falls and alarm
safety. They are source-context examples, not a current BC rulebook or
a claim that a historical annual goal list is still unchanged.

81
00:17:30,432 --> 00:18:17,856
Read the DSM structure as a reference framework: introductory purpose
and limits, diagnostic criteria grouped by related disorders,
assessment measures and models, cultural context, and supporting
appendices or codes. A criterion set is more than a category name: the
actual required symptoms, duration, impairment and exclusions must be
checked by an appropriately trained clinician. This assigned chapter
explains the framework and gives selected categories; it does not
print every disorder's complete diagnostic criteria. Recognizing
panic-like symptoms in a scenario therefore does not justify inventing
a criterion checklist or dismissing hypoxemia, ischemia, intoxication
or another concurrent cause.

82
00:18:17,856 --> 00:19:03,288
At the literal page ending, older adults may present differently and
need an individualized baseline. Bereavement, mobility loss,
isolation, cognitive change, mood, sleep, appetite and substance or
medication history can matter; age alone proves none of them. New
inattention or confusion during breathlessness needs assessment for
acute medical causes rather than automatic attribution to dementia or
depression. Preserve the patient's own report, observed changes,
communication needs and collateral history, then hand over what is
known and uncertain. The assigned page ends while this life-stage
discussion continues; no unseen continuation is claimed read here.

83
00:19:03,288 --> 00:19:59,760
The wound-care literal ending teaches skin-edge support, patient
education, hand hygiene and documentation after removal. Explain
gentle cleaning and drying, protection from strain and recognition of
separation, redness, swelling, discharge or other concerning changes.
The source expects adhesive strips to loosen naturally and gives one-
to-three weeks as an example, not a universal product-removal
deadline. Advice about fluid intake, activity and bathing must fit the
actual wound instructions and cardiac/renal restrictions. The next
checklist begins continuous/blanket stitch removal with identity,
consent, healing-risk/PPE checks and a valid order; this assigned page
does not contain the remaining removal sequence. Do not promise a
painless procedure or imply independent PCP suture-removal authority.

84
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:49,752
An implanted venous port has a reservoir beneath the skin joined to a
catheter; it is not simply a visible peripheral cannula. Access uses
the correct non-coring needle and specifically trained staff. Confirm
the actual device, intended route, access status, reason and insertion
history; the source also mentions devices terminating outside the
vascular system, so a port-shaped object alone does not establish an
IV route. Check dressing, securement, pain/redness/swelling, connected
cap and tubing, each infusate, fever and respiratory/circulatory
signs. This supports safe observation and handover, not puncturing an
unfamiliar port or starting central-line therapy after reading a
paragraph.

85
00:20:49,752 --> 00:21:34,776
A multilumen catheter contains separate internal paths with different
exit locations. Trace each labelled external line to its actual lumen
and prescribed infusate; assess each lumen separately. Separation
inside the catheter is not proof that every drug pair, connector or
shared segment is compatible. The source explanation of dilution in
blood does not replace a real compatibility check. During the weekly
transfer, a secure-looking triple-lumen line may still have one
disconnected, clamped or wrongly connected branch. Protect the
existing system, avoid traction and report the affected path rather
than assuming the whole catheter is working because another channel
flows.

86
00:21:34,776 --> 00:22:23,760
The source contrasts an open distal tip with a pressure-activated
valved design: in the illustrated valve, neutral pressure closes it,
aspiration opens it inward and infusion pressure opens it outward. It
associates open-ended devices with clamp and lock requirements and a
low-dose heparin example, while its valved example needs no clamp.
These are design examples, not universal instructions for every
catheter, connector or implanted port. Confirm the manufacturer and
agency-specific clamp/flush/lock sequence and solution before touching
the system; do not add heparin, omit a required clamp or force a
resistant flush from this comparison table alone. An untrained
operator should protect and escalate a compromised line.

87
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:34,656
Before moving, trace each relevant line and identify what it delivers
or drains. Plan oxygen supply and airway access for the actual move.

88
00:22:34,656 --> 00:22:45,744
Assign responsibility for devices and avoid traction, kinks or
unnecessary disconnection. The patient is not safely moved merely
because the stretcher is locked.

89
00:22:45,744 --> 00:22:56,952
Afterward repeat the primary observations and validate monitor
signals. A new poor number may be artifact, but it may also be the
first evidence of a real change.

90
00:22:56,952 --> 00:23:07,008
Treat credible threats while troubleshooting and record the fault,
correction and clinical response. Do not hide the event by deleting an
inconvenient reading.

91
00:23:07,008 --> 00:23:16,584
Use the school sixty-second target for a complete message, not a
rushed inventory. Lead with the patient and immediate combined threat.

92
00:23:16,584 --> 00:23:26,664
Give the important mechanism, symptom timing, current physiology and
interpretable ECG concern. Distinguish a witness report from a
confirmed cause.

93
00:23:26,664 --> 00:23:36,216
Report treatment actually provided and the response, including
continuing deterioration. Do not imply a drug was given because it was
considered.

94
00:23:36,216 --> 00:23:45,912
State ETA and the capability needed, then confirm the receiving plan.
Keep the detailed chronology available for the fuller handover.

95
00:23:45,912 --> 00:23:58,512
Receiving team, training unit Alpha with a sixty-one-year-old driver
after a high-energy collision. We have controlled major external
bleeding, but the patient remains poorly perfused.

96
00:23:58,512 --> 00:24:13,296
Current pressure is eighty-six over fifty, pulse one hundred twenty
and saturation ninety-five on supplemental oxygen. A useful twelve-
lead raises lateral ischemic concern; the pre-impact history remains
uncertain.

97
00:24:13,296 --> 00:24:25,992
Airway and ventilation are being supported as indicated, with repeated
primary assessment. We are continuing hemorrhage and perfusion care
without assuming that the ECG explains all shock.

98
00:24:25,992 --> 00:24:38,952
Estimated arrival is six minutes. We request the appropriate trauma
capability with urgent cardiac review. Please confirm the receiving
plan; we will update you immediately if support needs change.

99
00:24:38,952 --> 00:24:48,648
Record actual times for symptoms, ROSC, observations and treatment
rather than reconstructing a neat but fictional sequence later.

100
00:24:48,648 --> 00:25:01,824
Preserve ECGs, sampling context and monitoring limitations. A venous
oxygen number or poor waveform must not become an unquestioned
arterial or reliable result in handover.

101
00:25:01,824 --> 00:25:11,112
Document the working explanation, dangerous alternative, important
consultation and why a treatment was withheld, stopped or changed.

102
00:25:11,112 --> 00:25:22,056
Finish with the patient response, current support and transfer of
responsibility. Do not mark an unseen device checkoff or unperformed
school skill as completed.

103
00:25:22,056 --> 00:25:33,072
Reconstruct the actual timeline using observations and records.
Separate a technical setup success from a clinical response and from
observed hands-on competence.

104
00:25:33,072 --> 00:25:44,256
Identify where the team anchored, lost a monitoring task or failed to
hear a change. Include system and communication contributors rather
than blaming the patient.

105
00:25:44,256 --> 00:25:55,896
Select one measurable improvement, such as an earlier trend callout or
a more complete sixty-second alert. A faster message is not an
improvement if it omits the main threat.

106
00:25:55,896 --> 00:26:06,144
Retest the changed process with a new variation. Actual compression,
device fit and procedural competence still need the school observer
and real training equipment.

107
00:26:06,144 --> 00:26:16,080
You should now connect reliable diagnostics, acid-base patterns,
respiratory support and advanced ECG concern to one changing patient.

108
00:26:16,080 --> 00:26:25,944
Explain the primary versus complicating problem, verbalize a useful
What Else and evaluate what changed, what should change and what got
worse.

109
00:26:25,944 --> 00:26:37,008
Practise the original CO2-fall and medical-trauma sequences with the
bilingual reading, then deliver an accurate timed alert without
abandoning primary care.

110
00:26:37,008 --> 00:26:50,928
The remaining source gaps are explicitly recorded, including original
school slides and device rubrics. The next week follows the school
medical-neurological, endocrine, sepsis and gastrointestinal
requirements.
